A||||3||fierogt88||Coolant Draining||||1||
Z||000000||fierogt88||11-26-2000||05:43 PM||||I'm changing the coolant in my 88gt, but since I don't have a garage of my own, I wanted to check with you guys before taking it to my uncle's garage.  I don't have any manuals to help me right now (ah christmas list is forming... ) but I have searched and got the procedure for draining and refilling.  On first inspection, however, I don't see the radiator drain plug or the coolant pipe drain plugs.  Could anyone tell me where these are located and what i'll have to do to open them so as to save me some time in my uncle's garage?  TIA<P>------------------<BR>88GT Red 50k||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mark||11-26-2000||06:14 PM||||Radiator drain is at the bottom of the passenger-side tank. Twist with fingers or pliers.  Coolant line drain plugs are in each line, under the rocker panels and about a foot forward of the rear wheels (facing down).  They require an allen wrench.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||11-26-2000||06:26 PM||||The coolant drain plugs are screwed into the undersides of the coolant pipes on either side of the car.  The plugs take a 3/16" hex (Allen) wrench or socket, I believe.  The plugs are roughly 3" to 4" aft of the aft edge of the car door - you find them by feel, mostly - it's not difficult.  Remember that they unscrew CCW as viewed from <B>underneath</B> (you're viewing things from <B>above</B>, if car is on the ground, so keep that in mind).<P>If the hex recess in the plug is damaged, try a 5mm hex socket, and gently tap it in (carefully!) with the head of a sledge hammer.  A Torx bit might work, too (don't remember what size).  You won't have much room to swing the hammer head, and that's good!   <P>I use a hex socket+ ratchet.  To make things easier, spritz the plug w/ Liquid Wrench first.  Once the plug is loose, slide a pan underneath (I've used an old kitty litter box) to catch the coolant.  Remove the plug with the socket and your fingers.  After you drop in into the drain pan  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">, you can retrieve it with a magnet.  <P>Remove the thermostat housing cap (on the engine) using an oil filter wrench.  Pull the thermostat out, to allow air in while the coolant drains. <P>The radiator drain is on lower RHS of radiator, if I recall correctly.  You can open it with your hand, or a pair of slipjoint pliers.  Take off the radiator cap to let air in.           <P>Leave it to Stu to make a novel out of a simple job  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierogt88||11-26-2000||07:13 PM||||Thanks guys!  I have successfully located the coolant pipe drain plugs, but I have not found the radiator drain plug.  I have not jacked the car up, but I stuck my head under there and felt around the bottom of the right side.  Is it easy to see/reach with the car jacked up?  I also noticed that the front of my radiator has a lot of "squashed" fins.  There are no leaks but will this affect the operation?  should i start looking for a new radiator?  anybody have suggestions on how to protect those delicate fins from debris?||||reg||1
